## RELATIONSHIPS & ARCHITECTURE

### Dependency Graph

```
Controllers (HTTP Layer)
    ↓
Services (Business Logic)
    ↓
Repositories (Data Access)
    ↓
Entities (Domain Models)
    
Notifications
    ↓
NotificationManager (Config-driven resolution)
    ↓
NotificationChannel (Interface)
    ↓
LogNotificationChannel | EmailNotificationChannel
```

### Key Associations

**User ↔ Checkout (One-to-Many)**
- One user can have multiple checkouts
- Each checkout has one user

**Asset ↔ Checkout (One-to-Many)**
- One asset can have multiple checkout records
- Each checkout references one asset

**Asset ↔ User (through Checkout)**
- Assets track current holder (checked_out_by field)
- Users track their active checkouts

**Services ↔ Repositories (Dependency Injection)**
- Services depend on repository interfaces (not concrete classes)
- Allows swapping file-based for database-based repositories

**CheckoutService ↔ AssetService (Composition)**
- CheckoutService uses AssetService to validate asset state
- Ensures business rules (cannot checkout retired assets, etc.)

**AuthGuard ↔ Session**
- AuthGuard provides static utility methods for auth checks
- Consults $_SESSION for current user context

---

## POLYMORPHISM: NOTIFICATION CHANNELS

**Interface-based Resolution:**
```
NotificationManager implements config-driven driver selection:
- Config: driver = "log" → resolves to LogNotificationChannel
- Config: driver = "email" → resolves to EmailNotificationChannel
- Both implement NotificationChannel interface
- send(message) method polymorphic across implementations
```

**Extensibility:**
- Add new notification type: create class implementing NotificationChannel
- Update NotificationManager::resolveDriver("sms") → SmsNotificationChannel
- No changes to business logic layer required (Open/Closed Principle)


